WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:04.633
The best way
to experience the Speech API

00:00:04.633 --> 00:00:06.533
is with a demo.
Before I get into it,

00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:08.800
I want to explain a bit
how it works.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.300
So we're going to make
a recording,

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.300
and I wrote a Bash script.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.866
We're gonna use SoX to do that.

00:00:13.866 --> 00:00:16.733
It's a command line utility
for audio.

00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:18.700
So what we'll do
is we'll record our audio,

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.033
we'll create an API request
in a JSON file,

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.866
we'll send it to the Speech API,

00:00:22.866 --> 00:00:25.033
and then we'll see
the JSON response.

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:28.433
So if we could go ahead
and switch to the demo...

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:34.266
Okay, let me make the font
a little bigger.

00:00:34.266 --> 00:00:38.200
So I'm gonna call my file
"if Bash requests.sh."

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.733
And it says, "Press enter
when you're ready to record."

00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:44.166
It's gonna ask me to record
a five-second audio file.

00:00:44.166 --> 00:00:47.533
So here we go.

00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:52.866
I built a Cloud Speech API demo
using SoX.

00:00:52.866 --> 00:00:55.400
Okay, so this is
the JSON request file

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.800
that is just created.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.433
We need to tell the Speech API
the encoding type.

00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.766
In this case,
we're using FLAC encoding.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.840
The sample rate in hertz.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.260
The language code is optional.

00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:07.020
If you leave it out,
it'll default to English.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.733
Otherwise, you need
to tell it what language

00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:10.566
your audio is in.

00:01:10.566 --> 00:01:12.300
And then the "speech context,"
I'm gonna talk about that

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.033
in a little bit.

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:15.966
So I'm gonna call
the Speech API.

00:01:15.966 --> 00:01:18.133
It's making a cURL request.

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:21.800
And let's see how it did.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.566
Okay, so it did pretty good.
It said,

00:01:23.566 --> 00:01:26.200
"I built a Cloud Speech API demo
using socks,"

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.866
but you'll notice it got
the wrong "socks,"

00:01:27.866 --> 00:01:30.133
because SoX is a proper noun.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:33.166
It was 89% confident
that it got this correct.

00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:37.166
I even was able to get "API"
as an acronym.

00:01:37.166 --> 00:01:40.666
So I mentioned a speech context
parameter before.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:42.133
And what this
actually lets you do is,

00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:44.100
let's say you have a proper noun

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.800
or a word that you're expecting
in your application

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.500
that's unique
that you wouldn't expect

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.733
the API to recognize normally.

00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:53.066
You can actually pass it
as a parameter

00:01:53.066 --> 00:01:54.433
and it'll look out
for that word.

00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:57.300
So I'm gonna hop on over
to Sublime,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.800
and I'm gonna add "SoX"
as a phrase to look out for.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.266
And let's see if it's able
to identify it.

00:02:04.266 --> 00:02:07.300
I'm gonna say
the same thing again.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.766
I'm gonna record.

00:02:09.766 --> 00:02:15.700
I built a Cloud Speech API demo
using SoX.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.033
And we can see it's now got
that phrase in there.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:21.200
And we will call the Speech API.

00:02:25.266 --> 00:02:27.000
And it was able
to get it correctly

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.733
using the phrases parameter,
which is pretty cool.

00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:33.666
Just one REST API request,
and we are easily transcribing

00:02:33.666 --> 00:02:36.900
an audio file
even with a unique entity.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.266
And you can also pass
the API audio files

00:02:39.266 --> 00:02:40.766
in over 80 different languages.

00:02:40.766 --> 00:02:43.000
You just need to tell it, again,
the language code

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.866
that you'd like it
to transcribe.

00:02:44.866 --> 00:02:46.566
So that is the Speech API.

